{"about":"da6f74bed07c54a4f0de22cdecdce71da65119ad40ce1aa00214ae8c81c9aeca","body":"The conjecture claims that preferring better-corroborated theories is purely a methodological rule for future falsification, yet this fails to explain why scientists rely on these theories for high-stakes practical action (e.g., building bridges or administering medicine). If corroboration implies no increased probability of truth or future success, as Popper insists, then acting on the 'best' corroborated theory is indistinguishable from acting on a known falsehood or an untested guess. The preference only makes sense if one implicitly assumes that past survival under severe tests indicates a higher likelihood of future reliability, which smuggles in the very inductive principle the conjecture claims to avoid.\n[would settle: A demonstration of how a rational agent can consistently prefer Theory A over Theory B for life-critical decisions without assigning any higher subjective expectation of success to Theory A based on its test history.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}